course: biology
lesson: 153
title: Myth check: are bats blind?
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: People say «as blind as a bat», but every bat can see, and big fruit bats have large eyes that work well in dim light.
explain: Many small insect-eating bats have a second trick: they shout high-pitched calls and listen to the echoes, like a torch made of sound, which is called echolocation.
explain: On a summer evening over a pond you can watch bats swoop and twist after midges; each turn is a bat hearing an echo and snatching an insect it cannot easily see in the dark.
explain: Films show bats tangling in hair and vampires biting people, and Batman made the bat a dark, scary sign; real bats are shy, useful animals that eat pests, spread seeds and pollinate flowers.
fact: Bats are not blind; all bats can see, and many see quite well.
fact: Many small bats echolocate: they send out high calls and judge where an insect is, and even its size and shape, from the echoes; an echo from something close comes back sooner than one from far away.
fact: Big fruit bats called flying foxes have large eyes for dim light, and most of them do not echolocate at all.
fact: Bats do not get tangled in people's hair; they are very good at avoiding obstacles and may fly close only to catch insects around people.
fact: Real vampire bats are small, live in Central and South America, and mostly feed on the blood of cattle, horses and birds, rarely people.
fact: Bats hang upside down by their feet, and tendons lock their claws shut under their own weight, so hanging costs almost no effort.
fact: Bats take off by letting go and dropping into flight, because their legs are too small and light to jump up from the ground like a bird.
fact: A bat's wing is its hand: long finger bones hold out thin stretchy skin, and the thumb is a little free hook.
fact: Bats are the only mammals that can truly fly, and about one in five of all mammal kinds is a bat.
fact: A tiny pipistrelle, lighter than a coin, can eat around 3,000 small insects in one night.
fact: Some bats feed on fruit and nectar and, like bees, carry pollen between flowers and spread seeds; the wild ancestors of bananas and the agave are pollinated by bats.
fact: Many moths can hear bat calls and dive or drop to escape, and some tiger moths click back at bats, which may confuse the bat's echolocation or warn that the moth tastes bad.
fact: In cold countries many bats hibernate, and waking them uses up their stored fat, so bat caves are closed to visitors in winter.
fact: In China bats have long been a sign of good luck, because the word for bat sounds like the word for good fortune.
exercise: Are bats blind? | No, all bats can see | Yes, all bats are blind | Only baby bats can see | Only bats in caves are blind
exercise: What is echolocation? | Sending out calls and listening to the echoes to find things | Seeing clearly in the pitch dark with big eyes that glow like a cat's | Smelling insects from far away | Feeling the wind with the wings
exercise: How can a bat tell how far away an insect is? | Echoes from close things come back sooner | Close insects smell stronger | It counts how many times the insect beats its wings each second | It guesses and hopes
exercise: Which bats mostly see their way and do not echolocate? | Big fruit bats called flying foxes | Tiny pipistrelles | Vampire bats | All bats echolocate
exercise: Do bats get stuck in people's hair? | No, they are very good at avoiding things | Yes, they aim for people's hair on purpose | Yes, but only long hair that blows in the wind | Yes, every summer evening they swoop down to tangle themselves in it
exercise: What do real vampire bats mostly feed on? | The blood of cattle, horses and birds | The blood of people who live in the towns and villages nearby | Ripe fruit and sweet nectar from flowers | Fish they snatch from the rivers at night
exercise: Where do vampire bats live in the wild? | Central and South America | Transylvania in Europe | All over Britain | Australia only
exercise: Why can a bat hang upside down while asleep without getting tired? | Tendons lock its claws shut under its own weight | It holds on with its teeth | Its feet are glued to the roof by a sticky liquid it makes | It wakes up every minute to hold tight
exercise: How does a bat take off? | It lets go and drops into flight | It runs along and jumps like a bird | It climbs to the top and jumps up | It pushes off the ground with its wings
exercise: What holds out the skin of a bat's wing? | Long finger bones | Long feathers | Long rib bones | Long toe bones
exercise: What makes bats special among mammals? | They are the only mammals that can truly fly | They are the only mammals that lay eggs and keep them warm in caves | They are the only mammals with fur | They are the only mammals that sleep
exercise: About how many insects can a tiny pipistrelle eat in one night? | Around 3,000 | Only around 3 | Only around 30 | Around 3 million
exercise: How do fruit and nectar bats help plants? | They carry pollen and spread seeds | They eat all the pests on the leaves | They dig up the soil around the roots | They keep the flowers warm at night
exercise: Why does a moth suddenly drop out of the air when a bat comes near? | It hears the bat's calls and dives to escape | It is struck by lightning from the storm clouds above the field | It falls asleep from fright | The bat sucks it down
exercise: Why are bat caves closed to visitors in winter? | Waking hibernating bats uses up the fat they need to survive | The bats wake up hungry and bite the people who visit the caves in winter | The caves flood every winter | Bats hunt people in winter
exercise: Why are bats a sign of good luck in China? | The word for bat sounds like the word for good fortune | Bats bring gold coins | Bats only fly on lucky days | Batman first came from China, so bats are a famous lucky hero there
test: «as blind as a bat, so it can't see me if i wave» What is right? | Bats can see, and many also find things by echoes | Right, bats see nothing at all | Right, but they can smell you | Bats can only see the colour red, so the rest of the world looks black to them
test: «a bat flew past my head, it was gonna get stuck in my hair!!» What is right? | It was most likely catching insects near you; bats are very good at avoiding things | Yes, bats aim for hair | Yes, it wanted to pull out some of your hair to build a nest in the roof | Yes, bats cannot steer
test: In a scary film a vampire bat flies into a bedroom and bites a sleeping person. What is true? | Real vampire bats are small, live in the Americas and mostly feed on farm animals and birds | That happens every night in Europe | All bats drink human blood | Real vampire bats are as big as eagles and fly to Europe every winter to feed
test: Batman wears a dark bat costume to look frightening. Which is true about real bats? | Most are shy and useful, eating pests and spreading seeds | All bats are dangerous to people | Bats only come out at Halloween | Bats are a kind of bird, just one with fur instead of feathers
test: A bat has a body, two wings and two feet. Five bats hang from a cave roof. How many wings? | 10 | 5 | 20 | 15
test: Each wing of a bat is a hand. Three bats spread their wings. How many thumbs are sticking out as hooks? | 6 | 3 | 12 | 30
test: What would happen if all the insect-eating bats near a farm disappeared? | Many more insects would eat the crops and bite people and animals | Crops would grow twice as fast | Nothing would change | The fruit trees would make much more fruit, because no bats would be eating it
test: «lol why's a bat hanging upside down, gonna get a headache» Why does it hang like that? | It can drop straight into flight, and its locked claws make hanging effortless | It is trying to see the world differently, upside down, just for fun | It fell and got stuck | It is too lazy to stand up
test: A boy in winter finds bats asleep in a cold cave and wants to wake one to look at it. What is the best answer? | Leave it; waking it burns fat it needs to live until spring | Wake it, bats like visitors | Wake it up, because then it will fly out and eat lots more insects for you | Wake it gently with a torch
test: A tiger moth makes clicks when a bat comes near. What might the clicks do? | Confuse the bat's echolocation or warn that the moth tastes bad | Call other bats to dinner | Help the moth see in the dark | Make the moth fly faster
test: How can a bat flying through a dark wood avoid a thin wire? | It hears the echo of its calls coming back from the wire | It sees the wire glowing | It smells the metal | It remembers exactly where the wire was from the night before
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the visual capacity of bats. | All species of bat possess sight, and numerous species see with considerable acuity | The members of the order are without exception entirely blind | Only juvenile bats are possessed of sight | Sight is present solely in bats inhabiting caverns
exam: Identify the principal means by which insectivorous bats determine the position of prey in darkness. | The emission of high-pitched calls and the interpretation of their echoes | The detection of the scent of insects at great distance by means of the large nose | The perception of heat emitted by the insects | The tracing of insects by the light of the moon alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of the common vampire bat? | It subsists principally upon the blood of cattle, horses and birds, and seldom upon that of human beings | It subsists principally upon the blood of human beings, whom it visits at night in the towns of Europe | It subsists exclusively upon fruit and nectar | It subsists principally upon fish taken from rivers
exam: Given that each of four bats possesses the customary number of wings, determine the aggregate number of wings. | Eight | Four | Sixteen | Twelve
